should either properly implement the async generator interface or raise an error
without pretending to be a generator | The method has `if False: yield 
StreamChunk()` which creates an unreachable code path that makes static analysis
tools think it's a generator when it's not |\n| Type checkers should see the 
method returns `AsyncIterator[StreamChunk]` | The unreachable `yield` tricks 
type checkers into thinking it's a valid generator |\n| The method should 
clearly communicate it cannot stream | The `if False:` construct is a hack that 
obscures the actual behavior |\n\n## Root Cause\nThe `_NullProvider` class is a 
fallback provider that raises `RuntimeError` when used. The 
`chat_completion_stream` method needs to be an async generator (returning 
`AsyncIterator[StreamChunk]`) to satisfy the `ModelProvider` interface, but it 
never actually yields anything - it only raises an exception. The developer 
added `if False: yield StreamChunk()` to make the function syntactically an 
async generator, but this creates dead code and confuses both humans and 
tools.\n\n## Fix Plan\nRemove the `if False:` guard and the unreachable `yield` 
statement. Instead, make the method a proper async generator that immediately 
raises the `RuntimeError`. This can be done by either:\n1. Keeping it as an 
async generator with `async def` and `raise` (which is valid Python)\n2. Or 
restructuring to avoid the unreachable code\n\n## Status: IN PROGRESS"})

